+# Contract: `tarx create` Command-Line Interface
+
+**Feature**: `002-create-command` | **Date**: 2026-11-19
+
+This document extends — and does not alter — the `001-tarx-cli` contract in
+[`../../001-tarx-cli/contracts/cli.md`](../../001-tarx-cli/contracts/cli.md). Everything that
+document says about `list` and `extract` remains exactly true: same operands, same options, same
+stdout, same diagnostics (D1–D14), same exit statuses (FR-023). Only the additions below are new.
+
+Everything here is observable by a caller and therefore directly assertable. Message wording is
+part of the contract only where marked **exact**; elsewhere the contract is the *content* (which
+path is named) and the stream it appears on.
+
+---
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m tarx list    ARCHIVE
+python -m tarx extract ARCHIVE DEST [--overwrite]
+python -m tarx create  ARCHIVE PATH... [--overwrite]      ← new
+python -m tarx [-h | --help]
+python -m tarx COMMAND [-h | --help]
+```
+
+Requires `src/` on `PYTHONPATH`. No other command names, no other options, no globbing beyond
+whatever the shell performs, no exclusion patterns, no compression levels, no appending to an
+existing archive.
+
+| Operand / option | Meaning for `create` |
+|------------------|----------------------|
+| `ARCHIVE` | Path of the archive to write. **Its name alone** decides compression (FR-005). It is the only thing `create` writes, and `create` never creates a directory (FR-027). |
+| `PATH...` | One or more files or directories, relative to the current directory. At least one is required. A directory contributes itself and everything beneath it, at every depth (FR-003). |
+| `--overwrite` | Consent to replacing an existing **regular file** at `ARCHIVE` (FR-011). It never removes a directory and never resolves a type mismatch (FR-012) — the same boundary it has in `extract`. |
+
+`-h` / `--help` prints usage to **stdout** and exits **0**. The top-level usage names all three
+commands, their operands, and `--overwrite` (FR-024).
+
+---
+
+## Compression
+
+| `ARCHIVE` ends in | Archive written |
+|-------------------|-----------------|
+| `.tar.gz` | gzip-compressed tar |
+| `.tgz` | gzip-compressed tar |
+| anything else — `.tar`, `backup`, `data.bin`, `.TAR.GZ` | plain, uncompressed tar |
+
+The suffix match is literal and **case-sensitive**: `PHOTOS.TAR.GZ` gets a plain archive. This is
+verifiable from the archive's own first two bytes (`\x1f\x8b` for gzip). It does not conflict with
+`list`/`extract`, which detect compression from *content* and so read either form regardless of
+name (`001-tarx-cli` FR-004) — the name is load-bearing only at creation time.
+
+---
+
+## Member names
+
+A member's name is its path relative to the current directory, `/`-separated, with:
+
+- no leading `./` — `create a.tar ./photos` stores `photos`, `photos/a.jpg`;
+- no trailing `/`, **including for directory members** — a directory is recorded by its tar type
+  byte, so `tarfile.getnames()` returns `photos`, not `photos/`;
+- no absolute component and no upward-traversing segment (such a `PATH` is refused outright, C6).
+
+Operand forms `photos`, `photos/`, and `./photos` are three spellings of one path and produce
+identical archives. An operand that normalises to `.` contributes its *contents* under their plain
+relative names and no member of its own, because `.` is not a name FR-004 permits.
+
+---
+
+## Member order (FR-026)
+
+Fully determined by the invocation and the source tree — never by the order the filesystem reports
+directory entries:
+
+1. `PATH` operands contribute in the order given.
+2. Within one operand, members are sorted by **full member name compared as text**, which also
+   places a directory immediately before the entries inside it: `d`, `d/a.txt`, `d/b/c.txt`,
+   `d/z.txt`.
+3. A member reachable through more than one operand appears **once**, in the position the first
+   such operand earned it (FR-020). Identity is the member name — so two names for one file on
+   disk are two members, while two routes to one name are one member.
+4. The archive being written is never a member of itself (FR-019).
+
+Archiving an unchanged tree with an unchanged invocation therefore produces the same member
+sequence every time (SC-012). Archive *bytes* are not claimed to be identical across runs; the
+contract is the member sequence.
+
+---
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+| Status | Meaning | Triggers for `create` |
+|--------|---------|-----------------------|
+| `0` | Requested work completed | FR-017; includes runs where only non-regular entries were skipped (FR-015) and runs whose file count is 0 (an empty directory) |
+| `2` | Unusable input | `ARCHIVE`'s parent directory missing or not a directory (FR-027); `ARCHIVE` exists and is not a regular file (FR-012); a `PATH` that does not exist (FR-013) or is unsafe (FR-014); an existing entry that cannot be read (FR-021); invalid invocation (FR-022); failure while writing (FR-021) |
+| `3` | Refused overwrite | A regular file exists at `ARCHIVE` and `--overwrite` was not given, **and** no class-2 problem is present (FR-010) |
+
+Exactly one status per run. When a run exhibits both classes, **2 wins over 3** (FR-018) — status 3
+means "re-run with `--overwrite`", which would be false advice when a `PATH` is missing.
+
+---
+
+## Stream discipline
+
+- Command **results** go to stdout and nothing else does (FR-017).
+- **Diagnostics** go to stderr, one problem per line.
+- Every failing run writes at least one non-empty stderr line and writes **nothing** to stdout
+  (SC-006) — including the mid-write failure of FR-021, which never prints the summary.
+- Paths are written with the exact bytes the filesystem reports
+  (`errors="surrogateescape"` on both streams), so non-UTF-8 names round-trip.
+
+---
+
+## Success (exit 0)
+
+stdout is exactly one line, and it is the **exact** template:
+
+```text
+created ARCHIVE with N files\n
+```
+
+- `ARCHIVE` is echoed **exactly as the user supplied it** — not normalised, not made absolute.
+- `N` is the number of regular-file members added. Directory members and skipped entries are not
+  counted.
+- The word `files` is **always plural**, including `N` = 0 and `N` = 1. The line is a fixed
+  template for scripts, not prose (FR-008).
+
+stderr carries one line per skipped non-regular entry (below) and is otherwise empty.
+
+**Example**
+
+```text
+$ python -m tarx create photos.tar.gz photos
+created photos.tar.gz with 2 files
+$ python -m tarx list photos.tar.gz
+photos
+photos/a.jpg
+photos/b.jpg
+```
+
+---
+
+## Skipped entries (does not affect exit status)
+
+Entries that the filesystem distinguishes from a regular file — symbolic links, device nodes,
+FIFOs, sockets — are not stored, whether found inside a named directory or named directly as a
+`PATH`. Each is named on stderr, excluded from `N`, and its presence alone keeps the status at 0
+(FR-015).
+
+```text
+tarx: skipping non-regular path 'photos/latest.jpg' (symbolic link)
+```
+
+Labels: `symbolic link`, `character device`, `block device`, `FIFO`, `socket`,
+`unsupported file type`.
+
+Symbolic links are **not followed** — never dereferenced into a second copy of the data and never
+used to descend outside the current directory. A file that merely carries more than one name on
+disk is **not** skipped: each name is stored and counted as a regular file in its own right with
+its own copy of the contents (FR-015). The `hard link` member type exists only when *reading* an
+archive, where `extract` continues to skip it unchanged.
+
+Skip notices are written only when the run succeeds. A failed run creates no archive, so nothing
+was left out of anything, and its stderr carries the problems instead.
+
+---
+
+## Phases
+
+1. **Validation** — check `ARCHIVE` and its parent, then every operand's safety and existence,
+   then walk every named tree in full and confirm every entry can be opened for reading.
+   Accumulate *all* problems. **No filesystem writes occur in this phase, not one byte and not one
+   directory.**
+2. **Write** — entered only if validation found no problem: open `ARCHIVE` (this is the moment it
+   comes into existence), then write each planned member in order, streaming file contents.
+
+If validation finds any problem, **no archive is created**, any pre-existing file at `ARCHIVE` is
+left byte-for-byte unchanged, and no directory is created anywhere (FR-016, SC-006).
+
+Validation deliberately traverses the whole source tree before the archive is opened, so an
+unreadable file deep in a tree is a clean refusal rather than a partial archive. The residual
+window is stated, not hidden: a genuine mid-write failure (a full disk, a revoked permission, the
+archive's directory removed mid-run) can leave an incomplete archive at `ARCHIVE`, which must not
+be assumed valid. The tool does not roll back (FR-021), matching `001-tarx-cli` FR-022.
+
+---
+
+## Diagnostic templates
+
+All lines are prefixed `tarx: ` and written to stderr. `{archive}` and `{path}` echo the operand
+exactly as the user typed it. These numbers are a **separate namespace** from `001-tarx-cli`'s
+D1–D14, which are unchanged.
+
+| # | Condition | Class | Template |
+|---|-----------|-------|----------|
+| C1 | `ARCHIVE`'s parent directory does not exist (FR-027) | 2 | `tarx: cannot create archive '{archive}': directory '{dir}' does not exist` |
+| C2 | `ARCHIVE`'s parent path exists but is not a directory (FR-027) | 2 | `tarx: cannot create archive '{archive}': '{dir}' is not a directory` |
+| C3 | A regular file exists at `ARCHIVE` and `--overwrite` was not given (FR-010) | 3 | `tarx: refusing to overwrite existing file '{archive}' (re-run with --overwrite)` |
+| C4 | `ARCHIVE` exists and is not a regular file — directory, symlink, FIFO, socket, device node (FR-012) | 2 | `tarx: archive path '{archive}' exists but is not a regular file` |
+| C5 | A `PATH` does not exist (FR-013) | 2 | `tarx: source path '{path}' does not exist` |
+| C6 | A `PATH` is absolute or resolves outside the current directory (FR-014) | 2 | `tarx: unsafe source path '{path}': resolves outside the current directory` |
+| C7 | An existing entry cannot be read — a file that cannot be opened, a directory that cannot be listed (FR-021) | 2 | `tarx: cannot read '{path}': {reason}` |
+| C8 | A non-regular entry was skipped (FR-015) — **not a problem**, emitted only on success | — | `tarx: skipping non-regular path '{path}' ({label})` |
+| C9 | `ARCHIVE` cannot be opened for writing after validation passed — revoked permission, read-only filesystem (FR-021, Principle II) | 2 | `tarx: cannot create archive '{archive}': {reason}` |
+| C10 | A filesystem or archive failure after writing began (FR-021) | 2 | `tarx: failed while writing member '{name}' to archive '{archive}': {reason}` |
+| C11 | Invalid invocation — missing `ARCHIVE`, no `PATH`, unrecognised option (FR-022) | 2 | `argparse`'s own usage/error output, unmodified |
+
+C3 reuses D10's wording verbatim, because `--overwrite` means the same thing in both commands.
+C1 and C9 share a prefix but are distinct: C1 is a refusal found by validation (nothing was
+attempted), C9 is an unexpected failure of the open itself (`{reason}` comes from the OS).
+
+`{reason}` is the OS error text, whitespace-collapsed to keep one problem per line.
+
+### Problem ordering (FR-018)
+
+Every problem found is reported, one line each, in this deterministic order:
+
+1. **Archive-path problems** first — at most one of C1, C2, C3, C4 (they are mutually exclusive
+   checks on the same path).
+2. **Source-path problems** next, in operand order. One operand contributes at most one
+   operand-level problem (C6 is checked before C5, and either stops further checks for that
+   operand).
+3. **Readability problems** (C7) last, in plan order: operand order outer, full-member-name order
+   inner.
+
+**Example of a mixed-class failure**
+
+```text
+$ python -m tarx create photos.tar.gz missing-dir
+tarx: refusing to overwrite existing file 'photos.tar.gz' (re-run with --overwrite)
+tarx: source path 'missing-dir' does not exist
+$ echo $?
+2
+```
+
+Status is 2, not 3, and the existing `photos.tar.gz` is byte-for-byte unchanged.
+
+---
+
+## Guarantees a caller can rely on
+
+| Guarantee | Source |
+|-----------|--------|
+| Every regular file beneath every named `PATH` is a member, under its relative path | FR-003, FR-004, SC-001 |
+| A stored file's bytes are identical to the file on disk when it was read | FR-006, SC-002 |
+| `N` equals the number of regular-file members the archive actually holds | FR-008, SC-003 |
+| Compression follows the name, and is verifiable from the archive's own bytes | FR-005, SC-007 |
+| Every archive `create` writes is accepted by `list` and `extract`, exit 0 | FR-009, SC-008 |
+| Without `--overwrite`, zero pre-existing files are replaced or modified | FR-010, SC-004 |
+| Any failure the tool detects by validation leaves no archive and creates no directory | FR-016, SC-006 |
+| `create` never creates a directory under any circumstances | FR-027 |
+| Success, refused overwrite, and unusable input are distinguishable by status alone | FR-018, SC-005 |
+| The same tree and invocation produce the same member sequence every time | FR-026, SC-012 |
+| Memory use is independent of file size; file contents are never held in memory | SC-010 |
+| Permissions, ownership and timestamps are **not restored** by `extract` | spec Assumptions (out of scope) |
+| `list` and `extract` are unchanged in every observable respect | FR-023, SC-009 |
